reading: mean_eligible is the width available to run in parallel
with no global barrier. 'counter bits' is what the K<M/2 rule
demands at that lookahead — the answer to 'is a byte enough'.

== R5. PARALLEL WIDTH — can this be batched? ==
Conflict-free batch: events sharing no cell write disjoint
memory, so they commute EXACTLY and may run simultaneously.
Selection is a local minimum over the conflict neighbourhood —
a pure function of state, so batches do not depend on threads.
 lookahead   rounds   mean batch   speedup vs serial   matches serial
     0.050   124486         8.47               8.47x   EXACT
     0.200   124486         8.47               8.47x   n/a
     1.000   124486         8.47               8.47x   n/a

reading: mean batch is how many events a GPU could retire per
round. It is the ceiling on any parallel speedup here, and it
must come with EXACT agreement against the serial run — a batch
that is merely close is a different scheme.

== R6. DOES THE WIDTH SCALE WITH N? (decides GPU viability) ==
A GPU needs thousands of independent work items. A maximal
independent set in a fixed-degree conflict graph is a constant
FRACTION of the events, so width should grow linearly with N.
If it saturates, no GPU can help and the answer is no.
      N   events   rounds   mean batch   batch/event frac
     48      182     9054         5.18            0.0285
     96      402    12695         8.32            0.0207
    192      827    14408        14.86            0.0180
    384     1676    16223        26.31            0.0157
    768     3376    18112        47.39            0.0140
   1536     6749    20588        83.48            0.0124

reading: a constant batch/event fraction with mean batch rising
linearly means the width is there at scale and a GPU port is
worth building. A falling fraction means the conflict graph is
too dense and the scheme is latency-bound whatever the hardware.

== R7. THREAD-COUNT INVARIANCE (the ratchet requirement) ==
 threads   bit-identical to 1 thread
       2   YES
       4   YES
       8   YES
      16   YES

reading: the selection rule is a pure function of state, so this
must read YES at every thread count. It is the property that lets
the battery compare byte-identical reruns on any machine.
